/** @page ppc64le PowerPC64 little-endian (ppc64le) builds

@section ppc64le_linux Linux userspace unit tests

The @c ppc64le architecture supports two platforms: @c ieee1275 (the
default, described below) and @c linux.  The @c linux platform builds
iPXE as a 64-bit little-endian ELFv2 Linux executable.  This allows
the unit test suite to be run on POWER, or on any other host using
user-mode QEMU.

The build requires a @c powerpc64le-linux-gnu cross-compiler and C
library.  On Debian and Ubuntu these are provided by the packages @c
gcc-powerpc64le-linux-gnu and @c libc6-dev-ppc64el-cross, and the
emulator by the package @c qemu-user-static.

@code

	make -C src CROSS_COMPILE=powerpc64le-linux-gnu- bin-ppc64le-linux/tests.linux
	qemu-ppc64le-static -L /usr/powerpc64le-linux-gnu src/bin-ppc64le-linux/tests.linux

@endcode

The @c -L option names the directory containing the target dynamic
loader and C library, and is not required when running natively on
ppc64le.  A successful run ends with the line "OK: all N tests
passed".

@section ppc64le_ieee1275 Open Firmware (pSeries) image

The @c ieee1275 platform builds iPXE as a little-endian ELF image for
IEEE 1275 Open Firmware, as found on QEMU @c pseries (SLOF).  It is
the default platform for the @c ppc64le architecture.  The build needs
only the @c powerpc64le-linux-gnu cross-compiler:

@code

	make -C src CROSS_COMPILE=powerpc64le-linux-gnu- bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.elf

@endcode

The image is linked at a fixed address, so the firmware loads it
without relocation.  SLOF loads it from a CHRP boot CD: the ISO needs
a @c /ppc/bootinfo.txt whose boot script uses backslashes in the path.
For example, with @c xorriso:

@code

	mkdir -p iso/ppc
	cp src/bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.elf iso/ppc/
	printf '<chrp-boot>\n<os-name>ipxe</os-name>\n' > iso/ppc/bootinfo.txt
	printf '<boot-script>boot &device;:\\ppc\\ipxe.elf</boot-script>\n' >> iso/ppc/bootinfo.txt
	printf '</chrp-boot>\n' >> iso/ppc/bootinfo.txt
	xorriso -as mkisofs -R -o ipxe.iso iso
	qemu-system-ppc64 -M pseries -m 1G -nographic -cdrom ipxe.iso -boot d

@endcode

iPXE prints its banner on the firmware console.  Press Ctrl-B at the
prompt to reach the iPXE shell.  There is as yet no network driver, so
only local commands (for example @c echo and @c sleep) are useful.

@subsection ppc64le_ieee1275_kernel Booting with the QEMU -kernel option

Passing the image to QEMU with @c -kernel does not work by default:
SLOF prints "Booting from memory..." and then raises a program
exception (vector 0x700) before iPXE produces any output.  The image
itself is not at fault.  QEMU does not load a @c -kernel ELF at its
physical address: it places the low 28 bits of each program header's
physical address at the machine's @c kernel-addr, which defaults to
0x400000.  An image linked at 0x400000 is therefore loaded at 0x800000
(QEMU reports "kernel_addr changed from 0x400000 to 0x800000"), and
SLOF enters it there.  The code is position-dependent, so it addresses
its data and zeroes its @c .bss at the link address, which overwrites
the running copy of the image.

Supporting the default load address would require a relocatable
image, which is not provided.  Setting @c kernel-addr to zero makes
QEMU load the image at its link address, and the image then boots to
the banner:

@code

	qemu-system-ppc64 -M pseries,kernel-addr=0 -m 1G -nographic \
		-kernel src/bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.elf

@endcode

*/
